  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1257 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nAnother 24 hours or so of above normal temperatures is in store, \nbefore a long wave trough sweeps through, bringing a return to near \nor below normal levels (mid 40 highs and around 30 lows) into the \nupcoming holiday weekend. \n\nThe trough will be accompanied by a progressive sfc cold front and a \nmodest increase in low level moisture (PWATS 1\" to locally 1.25\") in \nthe immediate vicinity of the front. Plenty of forcing exists with \nthe trough to squeeze out at least some measurable precip for the \narea mainly Sunday afternoon, with trends still suggesting an \noverall weakening trend as the area moves east of I-69 (partially \ndue to loss of peak heating). CAA will commence fairly quickly behind \nthe trough with limited (if any) chances for any lake effect impacts \ndespite very favorable Delta Ts settling in by Monday night as \noverall moisture will be quite low. \n\nDry conditions prevail into mid week with models still varying on \nstrength, timing and amount of phasing with a northern and southern \nstream trough as they move into the Plains. Main impacts still \nappear to remain to our SE, but solutions persisting with slgt chc \nto chc pops late week.",
